13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 27, 1864","place":"Pigeon Hill and Burnt Hickory Road, Georgia","result":"Logan’s XV Corps attack was repulsed; none of its three assault brigades entered the main Confederate line","facts":[{"label":"U.S. assault force","value":"More than 5,500 men in Walcutt’s, Giles Smith’s, and Lightburn’s brigades"},{"label":"Attack axis","value":"Burnt Hickory Road between Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill"},{"label":"Attack began","value":"8 a.m."},{"label":"Closest approach","value":"Within about 15 yards in part of Walcutt’s sector"},{"label":"Confederate skirmishers captured","value":"About 120 by the 46th Ohio"},{"label":"Attack ended","value":"By noon"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Logan’s XV Corps attack in blue against Pigeon Hill and the Confederate line in red.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm","image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain on June 27, 1864"},{"title":"Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill in 1864","caption":"This wartime drawing shows the high ground and Confederate artillery positions that overlooked Logan’s approach.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/","image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"1864 drawing of Confederate artillery positions across Kennesaw Mountain"},{"title":"Pigeon Hill today","caption":"The XV Corps brigades advanced on both sides of Burnt Hickory Road against Pigeon Hill and the ground immediately south of it.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.66%2C33.965%2C-84.59%2C34.015&layer=mapnik&marker=33.985%2C-84.627"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:17","audioTime":77,"title":"The U.S. assault began on June 27","detail":"Federal artillery opened on the Kennesaw line. Samuel G. French’s Confederate division held Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill."},{"time":"2:30","audioTime":150,"title":"McPherson assigned XV Corps the main northern attack","detail":"Skirmishers pressed Big Kennesaw while John A. Logan’s corps attacked Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill."},{"time":"2:58","audioTime":178,"title":"Morgan L. Smith selected three brigades","detail":"Charles C. Walcutt attacked on the left, Giles A. Smith in the center, and Joseph A. J. Lightburn south of Burnt Hickory Road."},{"time":"4:26","audioTime":266,"title":"The assault force numbered more than 5,500","detail":"The three brigades had marched several miles during the night to reach their starting positions."},{"time":"5:54","audioTime":354,"title":"Most soldiers learned the plan at 7 a.m.","detail":"Officers received their final instructions one hour before the assault. Giles Smith described his brigade’s assignment as a forlorn hope."},{"time":"9:19","audioTime":559,"title":"Walcutt attacked between the two heights","detail":"The 46th Ohio led as skirmishers, followed by the 103rd Illinois and 6th Iowa, with three regiments behind them."},{"time":"10:01","audioTime":601,"title":"Confederate lines created a crossfire","detail":"William W. Berry’s and Francis M. Cockrell’s brigades bent toward each other between Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill."},{"time":"10:48","audioTime":648,"title":"The 46th Ohio captured about 120 skirmishers","detail":"The regiment overran a Mississippi skirmish line and continued onto the southern slope of Little Kennesaw."},{"time":"11:20","audioTime":680,"title":"Dense vegetation broke up Walcutt’s formation","detail":"The leading regiments pushed through tangled timber and undergrowth before Confederate fire stopped them on the slope."},{"time":"11:58","audioTime":718,"title":"Parts of the brigade came close to the works","detail":"The 103rd Illinois stopped about 60 yards away. An officer of the 46th Ohio reported reaching about 15 yards before steep rocks blocked further movement."},{"time":"13:15","audioTime":795,"title":"Walcutt withdrew","detail":"The brigade fell partway down the slope, stayed under cover for several hours, and then returned to its starting line."},{"time":"15:17","audioTime":917,"title":"Giles Smith attacked Pigeon Hill","detail":"Six regiments advanced north of Burnt Hickory Road against Cockrell’s Missouri brigade."},{"time":"16:24","audioTime":984,"title":"Smith’s men drove in the skirmish line","detail":"The U.S. regiments crossed thick woods, reformed at the base, and climbed until concentrated Confederate fire stopped them."},{"time":"17:45","audioTime":1065,"title":"Cockrell requested reinforcements","detail":"At 9 a.m., French sent several regiments from Matthew D. Ector’s brigade because Cockrell faced both Walcutt and Smith."},{"time":"18:41","audioTime":1121,"title":"The 57th Ohio colors were recovered","detail":"Captain Alva Skilton retrieved the fallen flag while under fire and carried it down the slope."},{"time":"19:44","audioTime":1184,"title":"William Porter was killed","detail":"The 55th Illinois captain was killed on Pigeon Hill one month after his marriage."},{"time":"20:06","audioTime":1206,"title":"Americus V. Rice was severely wounded","detail":"The 57th Ohio colonel suffered several wounds. His right leg was amputated that evening, and he survived."},{"time":"21:59","audioTime":1319,"title":"Lightburn attacked south of Burnt Hickory Road","detail":"Six regiments in two lines advanced against Hugh W. Mercer’s brigade on the right of W. H. T. Walker’s division."},{"time":"23:36","audioTime":1416,"title":"Rough ground disrupted Lightburn’s lines","detail":"A creek, woods, ravines, swampy ground, and thickets slowed the brigade before it reached the Confederate skirmish pits."},{"time":"24:34","audioTime":1474,"title":"The 63rd Georgia was driven from its rifle pits","detail":"Lightburn’s lead regiments fought at close range and overwhelmed the outnumbered Confederate skirmishers."},{"time":"25:38","audioTime":1538,"title":"Lightburn entered open ground","detail":"Mercer’s infantry had a clear field of fire, while artillery on Pigeon Hill fired into the U.S. flank."},{"time":"26:48","audioTime":1608,"title":"Lightburn ended the attack","detail":"Infantry and artillery fire stopped the brigade before the main Confederate works, and it withdrew."},{"time":"27:57","audioTime":1677,"title":"Osterhaus added a supporting demonstration","detail":"A heavy skirmish line advanced south of Lightburn but did not press a full assault against Walker’s line."},{"time":"28:26","audioTime":1706,"title":"The XV Corps attack was over by noon","detail":"All three assault brigades had been repulsed. None secured a position in the main Confederate works."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"James B. McPherson","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"John A. Logan’s XV Corps"},{"commander":"John A. Logan","command":"XV Corps","subordinates":"Morgan L. Smith and Peter J. Osterhaus"},{"commander":"Morgan L. Smith","command":"2nd Division, XV Corps","subordinates":"Charles C. Walcutt, Giles A. Smith, and Joseph A. J. Lightburn"},{"commander":"Charles C. Walcutt","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"Attacked between Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill"},{"commander":"Giles A. Smith","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"Attacked Pigeon Hill north of Burnt Hickory Road"},{"commander":"Joseph A. J. Lightburn","command":"3rd Brigade","subordinates":"Attacked south of Burnt Hickory Road"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"William W. Loring","command":"Loring’s Corps","subordinates":"Samuel G. French’s division on Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill"},{"commander":"Samuel G. French","command":"French’s Division","subordinates":"William W. Berry, Francis M. Cockrell, and Matthew D. Ector"},{"commander":"Francis M. Cockrell","command":"Missouri brigade","subordinates":"Defended Pigeon Hill"},{"commander":"William H. T. Walker","command":"Walker’s Division, Hardee’s Corps","subordinates":"Hugh W. Mercer’s brigade"},{"commander":"Hugh W. Mercer","command":"Mercer’s Brigade","subordinates":"Held south of Burnt Hickory Road; 63rd Georgia served as skirmishers"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Morgan L. Smith","role":"Commanded the XV Corps division making the assault","side":"United States"},{"name":"Charles C. Walcutt","role":"Led the brigade attacking between Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Giles A. Smith","role":"Led the brigade attacking Pigeon Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph A. J. Lightburn","role":"Led the brigade south of Burnt Hickory Road","side":"United States"},{"name":"Americus V. Rice","role":"57th Ohio colonel severely wounded during Smith’s attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Samuel G. French","role":"Commanded the Confederate division holding Little Kennesaw and Pigeon Hill","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Francis M. Cockrell","role":"Commanded the Missouri brigade that held Pigeon Hill","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Hugh W. Mercer","role":"Commanded the brigade that stopped Lightburn south of the road","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"William Porter","side":"United States","role":"Captain, 55th Illinois Infantry","outcome":"Killed during the attack on Pigeon Hill"},{"name":"Americus V. Rice","side":"United States","role":"Colonel, 57th Ohio Infantry","outcome":"Severely wounded; right leg amputated; survived"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of U.S. attacks and Confederate positions at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"Logan’s attack moved against Pigeon Hill while other U.S. forces pressed the wider line.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"Theodore R. Davis drawing of Confederate artillery positions at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"A wartime drawing of the Confederate positions overlooking the northern attack.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 519 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/02/01/civil-war-podcast-episode-519/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ga015"},{"label":"National Park Service troop movements map","url":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Union order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/union-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Confederate order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/confederate-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress Kennesaw battlefield drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}]}}]}]
